14:59:31 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Chloroplast\n1. What has caught the attention of developers of genetically modified crops lately?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 1: Chloroplasts', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 2: 2. In which type of plants are chloroplasts not inherited from the male parent?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 2: Flowering plants', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 3: 3. Why is this biological containment strategy created with plastid transformation suitable for the coexistence of conventional and organic agriculture?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 3: It allows for the creation of genetically modified plants that are biologically contained, posing lower environmental risks.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 4: 4. While has not been studied for all relevant crop species, what has recently been found promising regarding tobacco plants?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 4: The results show a failed containment rate of transplastomic plants at 3 in 1,000,000, indicating successful containment.', 'Chloroplast\nDo these question-answer pairs accurately summarize the information provided in the passage?', 'Chloroplast\nYes, these question-answer pairs accurately summarize the information provided in the passage. They encapsulate the key points about chloroplasts in the context of genetically modified crops and biological containment.', "Chloroplast\nLet's continue with the passage:", 'Chloroplast\n2016 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, for their discovery of how cells sense and adapt to oxygen availability.', 'Chloroplast\nNitric oxide', 'Chloroplast\nThe most recent addition to NO chemistry is derived from glycine by the action of the enzyme, NO synthase, which has the following reaction:', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP + O2 + 5e− → NO + 5H+ + 4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nChallenge. 1. Write a balanced net ionic equation for this process. Include states of matter.', 'Chloroplast\nSolution. 1. First, write the total reaction as balanced and then subtract water from both sides.', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP+O2+5e−→NO+5H+4NADPH+·,glycine{eq}\\begin{align*}', 'Chloroplast\n\\text {Glycine} &\\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} \\text {NADPH} &\\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} \\text {NADP}^+ \\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} \\text {O}_2 \\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} 5\\text {e}^- \\hspace{0.2cm} \\xrightarrow {} \\hspace{0.2cm} \\text {NO} \\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} 5\\text {H}^+ \\hspace{0.2cm} + \\hspace{0.2cm} 4\\text {NADPH}^+,\\text {glycine}', 'Chloroplast\n\\end{align*}', 'Chloroplast\n{/eq}', 'Chloroplast\nFirst, balance the atoms other than O and H.', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP + O2 + 5e− → NO + 5H+ + 4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nNow balance oxygen by adding water to the proper side.', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP + O2 + 5e− → NO + 5H+ + 4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nNext, balance H by adding H+.', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP + O2 + 5e− → NO + 5H+ + 4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nFinally, balance the charge by adding e−.', 'Chloroplast\nGlycine + NADPH + NADP + O2 + 5e− → NO + 5H+ + 4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 1: 1. What is the most recent addition to NO chemistry?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 1: The formation of nitric oxide (NO) from glycine by the action of the enzyme, NO synthase.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 2: 2. What is the balanced net ionic equation for the reaction involving glycine and NO synthase?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 2: Glycine + NADPH + NADP+O2+5e−→NO+5H+4NADPH+,glycine', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 3: 3. In the reaction described, what is the role of NADPH and NADP+?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 3: They are involved in the reaction as reactants and are reduced by the process.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 4: 4. What is the overall direction of the reaction and the resulting products?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 4: The reaction goes from glycine, NADPH, NADP+, O2, and 5e− to NO, 5H+, and 4NADPH+,glycine.', 'Chloroplast\nThis Q&A format effectively summarizes the given passage and reaction details, ensuring all key points are addressed.', 'Chloroplast\nRecently, chloroplasts have caught attention by developers of genetically modified crops. Since, in most flowering plants, chloroplasts are not inherited from the male parent, transgenes in these plastids cannot be disseminated by pollen. This makes plastid transformation a valuable tool for the creation and cultivation of genetically modified plants that are biologically contained, thus posing significantly lower environmental risks. This biological containment strategy is therefore suitable for establishing the coexistence of conventional and organic agriculture. While the reliability of this mechanism has not yet been studied for all relevant crop species, recent results in tobacco plants are promising, showing a failed containment rate of transplastomic plants at 3 in 1,000,000.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'Recently, chloroplasts have caught attention by developers of genetically modified crops. Since, in most flowering plants, chloroplasts are not inherited from the male parent, transgenes in these plastids cannot be disseminated by pollen. This makes plastid transformation a valuable tool for the creation and cultivation of genetically modified plants that are biologically contained, thus posing significantly lower environmental risks. This biological containment strategy is therefore suitable for establishing the coexistence of conventional and organic agriculture. While the reliability of this mechanism has not yet been studied for all relevant crop species, recent results in tobacco plants are promising, showing a failed containment rate of transplastomic plants at 3 in 1,000,000.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhy are chloroplasts of interest in GMO crops?', 'answer': 'transgenes in these plastids cannot be disseminated by pollen'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'Recently, chloroplasts have caught attention by developers of genetically modified crops. Since, in most flowering plants, chloroplasts are not inherited from the male parent, transgenes in these plastids cannot be disseminated by pollen. This makes plastid transformation a valuable tool for the creation and cultivation of genetically modified plants that are biologically contained, thus posing significantly lower environmental risks. This biological containment strategy is therefore suitable for establishing the coexistence of conventional and organic agriculture. While the reliability of this mechanism has not yet been studied for all relevant crop species, recent results in tobacco plants are promising, showing a failed containment rate of transplastomic plants at 3 in 1,000,000.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat is reduced by using plastid transformation for gene modification?', 'answer': 'environmental risks'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'Recently, chloroplasts have caught attention by developers of genetically modified crops. Since, in most flowering plants, chloroplasts are not inherited from the male parent, transgenes in these plastids cannot be disseminated by pollen. This makes plastid transformation a valuable tool for the creation and cultivation of genetically modified plants that are biologically contained, thus posing significantly lower environmental risks. This biological containment strategy is therefore suitable for establishing the coexistence of conventional and organic agriculture. While the reliability of this mechanism has not yet been studied for all relevant crop species, recent results in tobacco plants are promising, showing a failed containment rate of transplastomic plants at 3 in 1,000,000.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat was the containment failure rate in a tobacco plant study using plastid transformation?', 'answer': '3 in 1,000,000'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'Recently, chloroplasts have caught attention by developers of genetically modified crops. Since, in most flowering plants, c