15:03:17 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Imperialism\nWhat was the term "Imperialism" introduced into the English language?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 1: The term "Imperialism" was introduced into the English language in the late 1870s.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 2:  Who was the British Prime Minister during this time when the term "Imperialism" was introduced?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 2: The British Prime Minister during that time was Benjamin Disraeli.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 3:  How did the supporters of "imperialism" use the term when it was first introduced?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 3: Supporters of "imperialism" such as Joseph Chamberlain used the term to indicate a policy of idealism and philanthropy.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 4:  In terms of the negative connotation, how were opponents of imperialism viewing the term?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 4: Opponents of imperialism viewed the term as characterized by political self-interest and capitalist greed.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 5:  What are some of the key thinkers who expanded the term "imperialism" with different perspectives in their respective theoretical frameworks?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 5: John A. Hobson and Vladimir Lenin are two key thinkers who expanded the term "imperialism" with different perspectives in their respective theoretical frameworks.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 6:  How did Lenin characterize "imperialism"?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 6: Lenin characterized "imperialism" as a natural extension of capitalism that arose due to the need for expansion of investment, material resources, and manpower.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 7:  Are the writings of Lenin still influential in contemporary discussions of imperialism?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 7: Yes, Lenin\'s works, particularly "Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism," exerted a significant influence on later Marxist conceptions of imperialism, making his writings relevant in contemporary discussions of imperialism.', "Imperialism\nQuestion 8:  How did Marxist theoreticians incorporate Lenin's ideas on imperialism into their theories?", 'Imperialism\nAnswer 8: Many Marxist theoreticians followed Lenin\'s work and expanded on his ideas, emphasizing the structural or systemic character of "imperialism."', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 9:  What are some of the different axes that have been used to conceptualize imperialism in the literature?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 9: The moral, economic, systemic, cultural, and temporal axes are some of the different axes that have been used to conceptualize imperialism in the literature.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 10:  How have these different conceptualizations of imperialism changed over time?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 10: The meaning of the term "imperialism" has shifted over time, reflecting an increased focus on the fact of power and changing societal sensibilities.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 11:  Who were some other theorists besides Lenin that have had a major influence on Marxist conceptions of imperialism?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 11: The passage does not mention any other theorists besides Lenin who have had a major influence on Marxist conceptions of imperialism.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 12:  According to the passage, what is the expanded view of imperialism as suggested by Marxist conceptions?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 12: Marxist conceptions of imperialism suggest that imperialism is a structural feature of capitalism, characterized by a need for expanding investment, material resources, and manpower.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 13:  How does the passage differentiate between different axes used to conceptualize imperialism?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 13: The passage differentiates between moral, economic, systemic, cultural, and temporal axes to conceptualize imperialism.', 'Imperialism\nQuestion 14:  How does the passage explain that people became more uncomfortable with the fact of power, specifically Western power, leading to a need for more careful conceptualization of imperialism?', 'Imperialism\nAnswer 14: The passage explains that the growing unease, even squeamishness, with the fact of power led to a need for more careful conceptualization of imperialism.', "Imperialism\nQuestion 15:  Why do you think it's important to keep an open mind when reading academic texts?", 'Imperialism\nAnswer 15: It is important to keep an open mind when reading academic texts because different perspectives and approaches can lead to a better understanding of the subject matter and encourage critical thinking.', 'Imperialism\nIn anglophone academic works, theories regarding imperialism are often based on the British experience. The term "Imperialism" was originally introduced into English in its present sense in the late 1870s by opponents of the allegedly aggressive and ostentatious imperial policies of British prime Minister Benjamin Disraeli. It was shortly appropriated by supporters of "imperialism" such as Joseph Chamberlain. For some, imperialism designated a policy of idealism and philanthropy; others alleged that it was characterized by political self-interest, and a growing number associated it with capitalist greed. Liberal John A. Hobson and Marxist Vladimir Lenin added a more theoretical macroeconomic connotation to the term. Lenin in particular exerted substantial influence over later Marxist conceptions of imperialism with his work Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism. In his writings Lenin portrayed Imperialism as a natural extension of capitalism that arose from need for capitalist economies to constantly expand investment, material resources and manpower in such a way that necessitated colonial expansion. This conception of imperialism as a structural feature of capitalism is echoed by later Marxist theoreticians. Many theoreticians on the left have followed in emphasizing the structural or systemic character of "imperialism". Such writers have expanded the time period associated with the term so that it now designates neither a policy, nor a short space of decades in the late 19th century, but a world system extending over a period of centuries, often going back to Christopher Columbus and, in some accounts, to the Crusades. As the application of the term has expanded, its meaning has shifted along five distinct but often parallel axes: the moral, the economic, the systemic, the cultural, and the temporal. Those changes reflect - among other shifts in sensibility - a growing unease, even squeamishness, with the fact of power, specifically, Western power.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Imperialism', 'context': 'In anglophone academic works, theories regarding imperialism are often based on the British experience. The term "Imperialism" was originally introduced into English in its present sense in the late 1870s by opponents of the allegedly aggressive and ostentatious imperial policies of British prime Minister Benjamin Disraeli. It was shortly appropriated by supporters of "imperialism" such as Joseph Chamberlain. For some, imperialism designated a policy of idealism and philanthropy; others alleged that it was characterized by political self-interest, and a growing number associated it with capitalist greed. Liberal John A. Hobson and Marxist Vladimir Lenin added a more theoretical macroeconomic connotation to the term. Lenin in particular exerted substantial influence over later Marxist conceptions of imperialism with his work Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism. In his writings Lenin portrayed Imperialism as a natural extension of capitalism that arose from need for capitalist economies to constantly expand investment, material resources and manpower in such a way that necessitated colonial expansion. This conception of imperialism as a structural feature of capitalism is echoed by later Marxist theoreticians. Many theoreticians on the left have followed in emphasizing the structural or systemic character of "imperialism". Such writers have expanded the time period associated 