e into German in 1522 and completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, releasing the whole Bible in 1534.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2: Why did Luther tailor his translation of the Bible to his own doctrine?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: Luther tailored his translation of the Bible to his own doctrine because he wanted to communicate his unique theological ideas and principles through the text, including his beliefs about justification by faith and his rejection of works-based righteousness.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 3: How did Luther defend his insertion of the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 3: Luther defended his insertion of "alone" by emphasizing that the text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand this word. He argued that by including "alone," he was highlighting the importance of faith alone in justification, which was a crucial aspect of his doctrine.', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 4: What was the unique approach of Luther's translation of the Bible compared to other translations?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: Luther's translation of the Bible was unique in that he tailored it to his own doctrine, incorporating his personal beliefs and teachings into the translation. This led to a distinct interpretation of the Bible that differed from other translations and had a significant impact on the development of Protestant theology.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 5: What were the broader implications of Luther's translation of the Bible into German?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 5: The broader implications of Luther's translation of the Bible into German were far-reaching, including increased literacy among the German-speaking population, the dissemination of Luther's doctrine, and the promotion of the vernacular language as a medium for religious communication. This translated version of the Bible became a foundation for further translations into other languages and had a profound influence on the development of Christianity in Europe.", 'Martin Luther\nLuther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not works\'."'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Luther had published his German translation of the New Testament in 1522, and he and his collaborators completed the translation of the Old Testament in 1534, when the whole Bible was published. He continued to work on refining the translation until the end of his life. Others had translated the Bible into German, but Luther tailored his translation to his own doctrine. When he was criticised for inserting the word "alone" after "faith" in Romans 3:28, he replied in part: "[T]he text itself and the meaning of St. Paul urgently require and demand it. For in that very passage he is dealing with the main point of Christian doctrine, namely, that we are justified by faith in Christ without any works of the Law. ... But when works are so completely cut away – and that must mean that faith alone justifies – whoever would speak plainly and clearly about this cutting away of works will have to say, \'Faith alone justifies us, and not work