15:08:36 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["French and Indian War\nIn the spring of 1753, who was given command of a 2,000-man force of Troupes de la Marine and Indians and his orders were to protect the King's land in the Ohio Valley from the British?", 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 1: In the spring of 1753, Paul Marin de la Malgue was given command of a 2,000-man force of Troupes de la Marine and Indians.', "French and Indian War\nQuestion 2: What was Paul Marin de la Malgue's main objective and what instructions did he follow based on Céloron's previous expedition?", "French and Indian War\nAnswer 2: Paul Marin de la Malgue's main objective was to protect the King's land in the Ohio Valley from the British. He followed the route that Céloron had mapped out four years earlier, but where Céloron had limited the record of French claims to the burial of lead plates, Marin constructed and garrisoned forts.", 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 3: Can you describe the initial actions that Paul Marin de la Malgue took upon following the route mapped out by Céloron?', "French and Indian War\nAnswer 3: Paul Marin de la Malgue first constructed Fort Presque Isle on Lake Erie's south shore and then had a road built to the headwaters of LeBoeuf Creek. He also constructed a second fort at Fort Le Boeuf (present-day Waterford, Pennsylvania), which was designed to guard the headwaters of LeBoeuf Creek.", 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 4: How did Paul Marin de la Malgue handle his encounters with British traders and the Iroquois during his mission?', 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 4: Paul Marin de la Malgue drove off or captured British traders, which alarmed both the British and the Iroquois. Additionally, he drove the Iroquois westward as they were considered his adversaries.', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 5: Who was Tanaghrisson, and what was his stance on the French presence in the region?', 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 5: Tanaghrisson was a chief of the Mingo, who were remnants of Iroquois and other tribes driven west by colonial expansion. He intensely disliked the French and accused them of killing and eating his father. This hostility led him to threat the French with military action, which was dismissed by Marin.', "French and Indian War\nIn the spring of 1753, Paul Marin de la Malgue was given command of a 2,000-man force of Troupes de la Marine and Indians. His orders were to protect the King's land in the Ohio Valley from the British. Marin followed the route that Céloron had mapped out four years earlier, but where Céloron had limited the record of French claims to the burial of lead plates, Marin constructed and garrisoned forts. He first constructed Fort Presque Isle (near present-day Erie, Pennsylvania) on Lake Erie's south shore. He had a road built to the headwaters of LeBoeuf Creek. Marin constructed a second fort at Fort Le Boeuf (present-day Waterford, Pennsylvania), designed to guard the headwaters of LeBoeuf Creek. As he moved south, he drove off or captured British traders, alarming both the British and the Iroquois. Tanaghrisson, a chief of the Mingo, who were remnants of Iroquois and other tribes who had been driven west by colonial expansion. He intensely disliked the French (whom he accused of killing and eating his father). Traveling to Fort Le Boeuf, he threatened the French with military action, which Marin contemptuously dismissed."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'French and Indian War', 'context': "In the spring of 1753, Paul Marin de la Malgue was given command of a 2,000-man force of Troupes de la Marine and Indians. His orders were to protect the King's land in the Ohio Valley from the British. Marin followed the route that Céloron had mapped out four years earlier, but where Céloron had limited the record of French claims to the burial of lead plates, Marin constructed and garrisoned forts. He first constructed Fort Presque Isle (near present-day Erie, Pennsylvania) on Lake Erie