15:10:59 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Fresno, California\nWhat area of retail businesses and residences in California experienced a renewal in the late 1960s and 1970s?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: Fresno, California', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: What caused the decline of the neighborhood in the late 1960s and 1970s?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: A significant decline in the late 1960s and 1970s.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: When did the revitalization of the Fresno area begin after the neighborhood decline?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: The revitalization of the Fresno area began after the significant decline in the late 1960s and 1970s.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: What event is credited with starting the neighborhood revival?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: The re-opening of the Tower Theatre in the late 1970s, which at that time showed second and third run movies, along with classic films.', "Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: In what year did Roger Rocka's Dinner Theater & Good Company Players open nearby to the Tower Theatre?", "Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: Roger Rocka's Dinner Theater & Good Company Players opened in 1978.", "Fresno, California\nQuestion 6: What is Fresno native Audra McDonald's connection to the Tower District in Fresno?", "Fresno, California\nAnswer 6: Fresno native Audra McDonald performed in the leading roles of Evita and The Wiz at the Tower District's Good Company Players' 2nd Space Theatre while she was a high school student, and later became a leading performer on Broadway in New York City and a Tony award-winning actress.", 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 7: Which theatre in the Tower District is credited with being a 2nd Space Theatre?', "Fresno, California\nAnswer 7: Good Company Players' 2nd Space Theatre is credited with being a 2nd Space Theatre in the Tower District.", 'Fresno, California\nThe Towers Downtown Fresno, CA', "Fresno, California\nThe Towers was renovated from the Tower Theatre. The theatre was condemned in 1980 and had plans for it to be torn down to make way for a new convention center. After substantial fundraising, the movie theater was instead purchased by Ralph Thomas who had his original plan of reopening it as a viable movie theater and maintaining a close relationship with The Tower Theatre. In addition to a multitude of movies, it has also displayed a variety of art exhibits and hosted a diverse selection of live music shows, concerts and plays. In the early 1990s, Channel 3's KSEE Summer Concert Series were hosted at the theatre in the summer nights.", 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: In what year did the Tower Theatre in Fresno experience its condemnation?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: The Tower Theatre was condemned in 1980.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: What was initially planned for the Tower Theatre in Fresno after its condemnation in 1980?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: Initially, it was planned to be torn down to make way for a new convention center.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: Who was the individual responsible for purchasing the Tower Theatre after it was condemned in 1980?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: Ralph Thomas purchased the Tower Theatre after it was condemned in 1980.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: What is the current purpose of the Towers Downtown in Fresno after Ralph Thomas acquired it?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: The Towers Downtown is currently used as a venue for displaying art exhibits, hosting live music shows, concerts, and plays, and a location for KSEE Summer Concert Series.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: Are there any other notable events that took place at the Towers Downtown in Fresno during the early 1990s?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: Yes, the Towers Downtown hosted the KSEE Summer Concert Series during the early 1990s in the summer nights.', 'Fresno, California\nLos Angeles, California', 'Fresno, California\nThe Towers was originally the theater section of "The Tower Theatre & Restaurant" in Fresno. Originally built in 1919, this theater later became a bingo hall. In 1981, the name was changed to "The Towers". The name was kept, but it was abandoned by the late 1990s. The theatre was demolished in 2004.[citation needed]', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: What was the original name of the theatre in Fresno?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: The original name of the theatre in Fresno was "The Tower Theatre & Restaurant."', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: When was the original theatre built in Fresno?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: The original theatre was built in 1919.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: How did the theatre in Fresno change in the 1980s?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: The theatre later became a bingo hall in the 1980s.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: What year was the name of the theatre in Fresno changed to "The Towers"?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: The name was changed to "The Towers" in 1981.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: When did the original Tower Theatre in Fresno close down?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: The original Tower Theatre in Fresno was abandoned by the late 1990s.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 6: When did the Towers in Fresno get demolished?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 6: The Towers in Fresno was demolished in 2004.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 7: What was the initial purpose of the Towers Theatre in Fresno?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 7: The initial purpose of the Towers Theatre in Fresno was as a combined theatre and restaurant, but the theatre section became a bingo hall in the 1980s.', 'Fresno, California\nNew York City, New York', 'Fresno, California\nThe Towers was originally located in Times Square on Broadway between 47th Street and 48th Street. It was built in 1925 and opened in November of that year. It was designed to seat 2,800 people and was one of the first Times Square theaters to feature air-conditioning. It opened with the film Debutante, starring Lois Wilson and Nils Asther. During the early 1930s, The Towers featured big-name attractions such as the Monte Carlo Revue and a long-running Peking acrobatic troupe.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 1: Where was the original location of The Towers Theatre?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 1: The original location of The Towers Theatre was in Times Square on Broadway between 47th Street and 48th Street.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 2: When was the original The Towers Theatre built and when did it open?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 2: The original The Towers Theatre was built in 1925 and opened in November of that year.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 3: How many people could the original The Towers Theatre seat?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 3: The original The Towers Theatre was designed to seat 2,800 people.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 4: What was a unique feature of the original The Towers Theatre?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 4: The original The Towers Theatre featured air-conditioning, which made it one of the first Times Square theaters to do so.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 5: What film did the original The Towers Theatre open with?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 5: The original The Towers Theatre opened with the film "Debutante," starring Lois Wilson and Nils Asther.', 'Fresno, California\nQuestion 6: During the early 1930s, what kind of attractions did The Towers Theatre feature?', 'Fresno, California\nAnswer 6: During the early 1930s, The Towers Theatre featured big-name attractions such as the Monte Carlo Revue and a long-running Peking acrobatic troupe.', "Fresno, California\nThis vibrant and culturally diverse area of retail businesses and residences experienced a renewal after a significant decline in the late 1960s and 1970s.[citation needed] After decades of neglect and suburban flight, the neighborhood revival followed the re-opening of the Tower Theatre in the late 1970s, which at that time showed second and third run movies, along with classic films. Roger Rocka's Dinner Theater & Good Company Players also opened nearby in 1978,[citation needed] at Olive and Wishon Avenues. Fresno native Audra McDonald performed in the leading roles of Evita and The Wiz at the theater while she was a high school student. McDonald subsequently became a leading performer on Broadway in New York City and a Tony award winning actress. Also in the Tower District is Good Company Players' 2nd Space Theatre."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "This vibrant and culturally diverse area of retail businesses and residences experienced a renewal after a significant decline in the late 1960s and 1970s.[citation needed] After decades of neglect and suburban flight, the neighborhood revival followed the re-opening of the Tower Theatre in the late 1970s, which at that time showed second and third run movies, along with classic films. Roger Rocka's Dinner Theater & Good Company Players also opened nearby in 1978,[citation needed] at Olive and Wishon Avenues. Fresno native Audra McDonald performed in the leading roles of Evita and The Wiz at the theater while she was a high school student. McDonald subsequently became a leading performer on Broadway in New York City and a Tony award winning actress. Also in the Tower District is Good Company Players' 2nd Space Theatre.", 'question': 'Topic: Fresno, California\nDuring what period did the Tower Theatre re-open?', 'answer': 'late 1970s'}, {'title': 'Fresno, California', 'context': "This vibrant and culturally diverse area of retail businesses and residences experienced a renewal after a significant decline in the late 1960s and 1970s.[citation needed] After decades of neglect and suburban flight, the neighborhood revival followed the re-opening of the Tower Theatre in the late 1970s, which at that time showed second and third run movies, along with classic films. Roger Rocka's Dinner Theater & Good Company Players also opened nearby in 1978,[cit