15:17:13 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Chloroplast\nwhat is the role of thylakoids in plant cells?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 1:   In plant cells, the primary role of the thylakoids is as part of the photosynthesis process. The thylakoids contain the chlorophyll and other pigments necessary for capturing sunlight and converting it into chemical energy. The ATP and NADPH produced by the process are used to power the conversion of CO2 into organic compounds.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 2: How is ATP and NADPH produced in the thylakoids?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 2:   In the thylakoids, ATP and NADPH are produced through a process called the light-dependent reactions. The light energy from the sun is absorbed by the chlorophyll in the thylakoid membrane and converted into ATP and NADPH.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 3:  What is the role of the thylakoids in the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 3:   In the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis, the thylakoids help capture light energy from the sun, convert it into chemical energy in the form of ATP and NADPH, and facilitate the electron transport chain that transports electrons and protons.', 'Chloroplast\nQuestion 4: How do the ATP and NADPH produced by the light-dependent reactions of photosynthesis get used in the light-independent reactions?', 'Chloroplast\nAnswer 4:  After the light-dependent reactions, the ATP and NADPH produced are used in the light-independent reactions, also known as the Calvin cycle. In this process, they are used to drive the conversion of CO2 into organic compounds like glucose.', 'Chloroplast\nWhile photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat usually takes reenergized electrons?', 'answer': 'NADP+'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nIn what process are electrons recycled?', 'answer': 'cyclic photophosphorylation'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhere is cyclic photophosphorylation common?', 'answer': 'in C4 plants'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': 'While photosystem II photolyzes water to obtain and energize new electrons, photosystem I simply reenergizes depleted electrons at the end of an electron transport chain. Normally, the reenergized electrons are taken by NADP+, though sometimes they can flow back down more H+-pumping electron transport chains to transport more hydrogen ions into the thylakoid space to generate more ATP. This is termed cyclic photophosphorylation because the electrons are recycled. Cyclic photophosphorylation is common in C4 plants, which need more ATP than NADPH.', 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do C4 plants need?', 'answer': 'more ATP than NADPH'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
