e American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 7: Yes, the American Revolution played a significant role in the separation of Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 8: Who did John Wesley appoint as a bishop for the Methodists in 1784?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 8: John Wesley appointed Thomas Coke as a bishop for the Methodists in 1784.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 9: What year did the Methodist Episcopal Church officially establish?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 9: The Methodist Episcopal Church officially established in 1784 at the Baltimore Christmas Conference.', 'United Methodist Church\nQuestion 10: Which historical figure was responsible for the separation of the Methodists in the American colonies from the Church of England?', 'United Methodist Church\nAnswer 10: John Wesley was responsible for the separation of the Methodists in the American colonies from the Church of England.', 'United Methodist Church\nFor the following reaction, 64.2 grams of iron(III) oxide are allowed to react with 26.7 grams of carbon monoxide.', 'United Methodist Church\niron(III) oxide (s) + carbon monoxide (g) → iron (s) + carbon dioxide (g)', 'United Methodist Church\nWhat is the maximum amount of iron that can be formed?', 'United Methodist Church\nTo determine the maximum amount of iron that can be formed in the reaction, we need to follow these steps:', 'United Methodist Church\n1. **Write the balanced chemical equation:**', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 3\\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe}(s) + 3\\text{CO}_2(g)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n2. **Calculate the molar masses of the reactants and products:**', 'United Methodist Church\n- Molar mass of \\(\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n2 \\times 55.85 \\, \\text{g/mol} + 3 \\times 16.00 \\, \\text{g/mol} = 159.70 \\, \\text{g/mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n- Molar mass of \\(\\text{CO}\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n12.01 \\, \\text{g/mol} + 16.00 \\, \\text{g/mol} = 28.01 \\, \\text{g/mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n- Molar mass of \\(\\text{Fe}\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n55.85 \\, \\text{g/mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n3. **Convert the masses of the reactants to moles:**', 'United Methodist Church\n- Moles of \\(\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\frac{64.2 \\, \\text{g}}{159.70 \\, \\text{g/mol}} = 0.401 \\, \\text{mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n- Moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\frac{26.7 \\, \\text{g}}{28.01 \\, \\text{g/mol}} = 0.953 \\, \\text{mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\n4. **Determine the limiting reactant:**', 'United Methodist Church\nAccording to the balanced equation, 1 mole of \\(\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3\\) reacts with 3 moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\). Therefore, the required moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\) for 0.401 moles of \\(\\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3\\) are:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n0.401 \\, \\text{mol} \\times 3 = 1.203 \\, \\text{mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nSince we only have 0.953 moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\), \\(\\text{CO}\\) is the limiting reactant.', 'United Methodist Church\n5. **Calculate the maximum amount of iron that can be formed:**', 'United Methodist Church\nFrom the balanced equation, 3 moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\) produce 2 moles of \\(\\text{Fe}\\). Therefore, the moles of \\(\\text{Fe}\\) produced by 0.953 moles of \\(\\text{CO}\\) are:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n0.953 \\, \\text{mol} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 0.635 \\, \\text{mol}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nConvert moles of \\(\\text{Fe}\\) to grams:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n0.635 \\, \\text{mol} \\times 55.85 \\, \\text{g/mol} = 35.43 \\, \\text{g}', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nThus, the maximum amount of iron that can be formed is \\(\\boxed{35.43 \\, \\text{g}}\\).', 'United Methodist Church\nFind the equation of the plane that passes through the points $A\\left(-\\frac{7}{3},-\\frac{14}{3},-\\frac{11}{3}\\right)$, $B(4,-1,5)$, and $C\\left(\\frac{7}{3},-4,-3\\right)$.', 'United Methodist Church\nTo find the equation of the plane that passes through the points \\( A\\left(-\\frac{7}{3},-\\frac{14}{3},-\\frac{11}{3}\\right) \\), \\( B(4,-1,5) \\), and \\( C\\left(\\frac{7}{3},-4,-3\\right) \\), we need to follow these steps:', 'United Methodist Church\n1. **Find two vectors that lie in the plane:**', 'United Methodist Church\nWe can use the position vectors of points \\( B \\) and \\( C \\) relative to point \\( A \\).', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = B - A = \\left(4 - \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right), -1 - \\left(-\\frac{14}{3}\\right), 5 - \\left(-\\frac{11}{3}\\right)\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nSimplify each component:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\left(4 + \\frac{7}{3}, -1 + \\frac{14}{3}, 5 + \\frac{11}{3}\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nConvert to a common denominator:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\left(\\frac{12}{3} + \\frac{7}{3}, -\\frac{3}{3} + \\frac{14}{3}, \\frac{15}{3} + \\frac{11}{3}\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nAdd the fractions:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AB} = \\left(\\frac{19}{3}, \\frac{11}{3}, \\frac{26}{3}\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nSimilarly, find \\(\\overrightarrow{AC}\\):', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AC} = C - A = \\left(\\frac{7}{3} - \\left(-\\frac{7}{3}\\right), -4 - \\left(-\\frac{14}{3}\\right), -3 - \\left(-\\frac{11}{3}\\right)\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nSimplify each component:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AC} = \\left(\\frac{7}{3} + \\frac{7}{3}, -4 + \\frac{14}{3}, -3 + \\frac{11}{3}\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nConvert to a common denominator:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\n\\overrightarrow{AC} = \\left(\\frac{14}{3}, -\\frac{12}{3} + \\frac{14}{3}, -\\frac{9}{3} + \\frac{11}{3}\\right)', 'United Methodist Church\n\\]', 'United Methodist Church\nAdd the fractions:', 'United Methodist Church\n\\[', 'United Methodist Church\nThough John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Though John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat caused the separation of the Methodists in the American colonies?', 'answer': 'American Revolution'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Though John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhen did Wesley appoint Thomas Coke as bishop?', 'answer': '1784'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Though John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWho did Wesley appoint as bishop in 1784?', 'answer': 'Thomas Coke'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Though John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhere was the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784 held?', 'answer': 'Lovely Lane Methodist Church'}, {'title': 'United Methodist Church', 'context': 'Though John Wesley originally wanted the Methodists to stay within the Church of England, the American Revolution decisively separated the Methodists in the American colonies from the life and sacraments of the Anglican Church. In 1784, after unsuccessful attempts to have the Church of England send a bishop to start a new church in the colonies, Wesley decisively appointed fellow priest Thomas Coke as superintendent (bishop) to organize a separate Methodist Society. Together with Coke, Wesley sent a revision of the Anglican Prayerbook and the Articles of Religion which were received and adopted by the Baltimore Christmas Conference of 1784, officially establishing the Methodist Episcopal Church. The conference was held at the Lovely Lane Methodist Church, considered the Mother Church of American Methodism.', 'question': 'Topic: United Methodist Church\nWhat is considered the Mother Church of American Methodism?', 'answer': 'Lovely Lane Methodist Church'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
