15:23:15 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\nWhen was Martin Luther ordained?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 1: Martin Luther was ordained in 1507.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2:  In what year did von Staupitz, the first dean of the University of Wittenberg, send for Luther to teach theology?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: 1508', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 3:  What degree did Martin Luther receive on 9 March 1508?', "Martin Luther\nAnswer 3:  Martin Luther received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508.", 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 4:  What degree did Luther receive in 1509?', "Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: Luther received a bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 5:  What position did von Staupitz hold?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 5: Von Staupitz was the first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 6:  What year was the University of Wittenberg established?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 6: The University of Wittenberg was established in 1508.', "Martin Luther\nCan you please provide me with more information about Martin Luther's life after his ordination in 1507 and his teaching at the University of Wittenberg?", 'Martin Luther\nSure! After his ordination in 1507, Martin Luther continued his studies at the University of Wittenberg under the guidance of his mentor, Johannes von Staupitz. In 1508, Von Staupitz, the first dean of the University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther to teach theology. Martin Luther taught theology at the university for many years until 1520, when he was fired from his position due to his opposition to the indulgences being sold by the Church.', "Martin Luther\nLuther's teachings and his critique of the Church led to the Protestant Reformation, which began in 1517 when he nailed his 95 theses to the door of the Wittenberg Castle church. He became a pivotal figure in the reform movement and wrote many works on theology and politics. Luther's writings had a significant impact on Western Christianity, leading to the emergence of Protestant denominations such as the Lutheran, Anglican, and Reformed traditions. Martin Luther's life after his ordination was marked by his activism and contributions to religious and political reform in Europe.", "Martin Luther\nCan you give me more specific details on the events and arguments that led to the Protestant Reformation under Martin Luther's influence?", "Martin Luther\nSure! Here are some specific details on the events and arguments that led to the Protestant Reformation under Martin Luther's influence:", 'Martin Luther\n1. Indulgences: One of the key catalysts of the Reformation was the sale of indulgences by the Catholic Church. Indulgences were certificates that supposedly reduced the time that a person would spend in purgatory after death. Martin Luther was particularly upset by the practice of selling indulgences, which he saw as nothing more than a way for the Church to raise money. In 1517, Luther sent a list of 95 theses, or grievances, to the Archbishop of Mainz, challenging the Church on its teaching and practices. The 95 theses included criticisms of the indulgence system and called for reform within the Church.', "Martin Luther\n2. Salvation: Another issue that concerned Martin Luther was the nature of salvation. Luther believed that salvation was a gift of God, given through faith alone, and that it could not be purchased or earned by good works. This belief challenged the Church's teaching that salvation could be attained through indulgences, pilgrimages, or other good deeds.", 'Martin Luther\n3. The authority of scripture: Luther also argued that the Bible was the ultimate authority on matters of faith and practice, rather than the teachings and traditions of the Church. He believed that the Bible was inspired by God and should be the sole source of religious authority.', 'Martin Luther\n4. Church corruption: Luther was also concerned about the moral corruption within the Church, which he believed had led to abuses of power and financial corruption. He saw the Reformation as a way to return the Church to its original mission of serving God and the people.', "Martin Luther\nTogether, these concerns and arguments led to the emergence of the Protestant Reformation, which sought to reform and challenge the authority of the Catholic Church. Luther's writings and teachings had a significant impact on Western Christianity, and his influence can still be seen in many Protestant denominations today.", "Martin Luther\nCan you tell me more about Martin Luther's personal life and how it influenced his teachings?", 'Martin Luther\nSure, I can certainly tell you more about Martin Luther\'s personal life and how it influenced his teachings. Martin Luther was born in Eisleben, Germany, in 1483. His father was a miner and blacksmith who hoped to provide a better life for his son by sending him to the University of Erfurt to study law. Despite this, Luther became interested in theology and began to study it alongside his law degree. In 1505, he entered the monastery of the Augustinian Order and took the name "Martin."', "Martin Luther\nDuring his monastic years, Luther struggled with his faith and his sense of sin. He was constantly haunted by the idea of God's judgment and feared that he was doomed to eternal damnation. This led him to seek out answers to his spiritual concerns, and he began to read and study the Bible more intently. This led him to develop some of his most important theological beliefs, including the doctrine of justification by faith alone and the idea that God's law is only meant to show us our sins rather than to provide a way of salvation.", "Martin Luther\nLuther's personal struggles with faith and sin also had an impact on his approach to preaching and teaching. He believed that the Bible should be accessible to all people and that everyone should be able to read it for themselves. This belief led him to translate the Bible into German, making it available to a wider audience.", "Martin Luther\nIn addition to his faith, Luther's personal life was also shaped by his family. He married Katharina von Bora, a former nun, in 1525. They had six children together, and their marriage was reportedly a close and loving one. Luther's commitment to family life and his role as a father and husband also influenced his teachings, which emphasized the importance of responsible family life and the role of children in the Christian community.", "Martin Luther\nIn summary, Martin Luther's personal life played a significant role in shaping his teachings and beliefs. His struggles with faith and sin, his family life, and his commitment to accessibility and justice all influenced his religious and political convictions.", "Martin Luther\nIn 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "In 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen was Martin Luther ordained as a priest?', 'answer': '1507'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "In 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWho asked Luther to teach theology at the University of Wittenberg?', 'answer': 'von Staupitz'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "In 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen was Luther  called by von Staupitz to Wittenberg?', 'answer': '1508'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "In 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen did Luther receive a degree in Biblical studies?', 'answer': '9 March 1508'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': "In 1507, he was ordained to the priesthood, and in 1508, von Staupitz, first dean of the newly founded University of Wittenberg, sent for Luther, to teach theology. He received a bachelor's degree in Biblical studies on 9 March 1508, and another bachelor's degree in the Sentences by Peter Lombard in 1509.", 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther's degree in 1509 concern?", 'answer': 'Sentences by Peter Lombard'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
