15:26:26 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Harvard University\nWhat is Harvard University?', 'Harvard University\nAnswer 1: Harvard University is a prestigious research institution.', 'Harvard University\nQuestion 2: Who was the president of Harvard University?', 'Harvard University\nAnswer 2: James Bryant Conant was the president of Harvard University from 1933 to 1953.', "Harvard University\nQuestion 3: What was James Bryant Conant's contribution to Harvard University?", 'Harvard University\nAnswer 3: James Bryant Conant revitalized creative scholarship at Harvard University to maintain its preeminence among research institutions. Additionally, he saw higher education as a tool for opportunity for talented students, regardless of their wealth. To achieve this, he devised programs to identify and support talented youth.', 'Harvard University\nQuestion 4: In what year did James Bryant Conant devise programs to identify talented youth at Harvard University?', 'Harvard University\nAnswer 4: James Bryant Conant devised programs to identify talented youth at Harvard University in 1943.', "Harvard University\nQuestion 5: What was Conant's statement regarding general education at secondary and college levels?", 'Harvard University\nAnswer 5: In 1943, James Bryant Conant asked the faculty to make a definitive statement about general education at both the secondary and college levels. The resulting report, published in 1945, had a significant impact on American education in the 20th century.', 'Harvard University\nQuestion 6: What was the impact of the report on general education at Harvard University?', "Harvard University\nAnswer 6: The report, published in 1945, was one of the most influential manifestos in American education in the 20th century, further solidifying Harvard University's reputation as a leading institution in education.", 'Harvard University\nJames Bryant Conant (president, 1933–1953) reinvigorated creative scholarship to guarantee its preeminence among research institutions. He saw higher education as a vehicle of opportunity for the talented rather than an entitlement for the wealthy, so Conant devised programs to identify, recruit, and support talented youth. In 1943, he asked the faculty make a definitive statement about what general education ought to be, at the secondary as well as the college level. The resulting Report, published in 1945, was one of the most influential manifestos in the history of American education in the 20th century.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Harvard University', 'context': 'James Bryant Conant (president, 1933–1953) reinvigorated creative scholarship to guarantee its preeminence among research institutions. He saw higher education as a vehicle of opportunity for the talented rather than an entitlement for the wealthy, so Conant devised programs to identify, recruit, and support talented youth. In 1943, he asked the faculty make a definitive statement about what general education ought to be, at the secondary as well as the college level. The resulting Report, published in 1945, was one of the most influential manifestos in the history of American education in the 20th century.', 'question': 'Topic: Harvard University\nWho lead the school back to leading research institution in 2oth century?', 'answer': 'James Bryant Conant'}, {'title': 'Harvard University', 'context': 'James Bryant Conant (president, 1933–1953) reinvigorated creative scholarship to guarantee its preeminence among research institutions. He saw higher education as a vehicle of opportunity for the talented rather than an entitlement for the wealthy, so Conant devised programs to identify, recruit, and support talented youth. In 1943, he asked the faculty make a definitive statement about what general education ought to be, at the secondary as well as the college level. The resulting Report, published in 1945, was one of the most influential manifestos in the history of American education in the 20th century.', 'question': 'Topic: Harvard University\nHow was the school able to bring aboard the best talented students?', 'answer': 'identify, recruit'}, {'title': 'Harvard University', 'context': 'James Bryant Conant (president, 1933–1953) reinvigorated creative scholarship to guarantee its preeminence among research institutions. He saw higher education as a vehicle of opportunity for the talented rather than an entitlement for the wealthy, so Conant devised programs to identify, recruit, and support talented youth. In 1943, he asked the faculty make a definitive statement about what general education ought to be, at the secondary as well as the college level. The resulting Report, published in 1945, was one of the most influential manifestos in the history of American education in the 20th century.', 'question': 'Topic: Harvard University\nIn what year of 20th century, did Harvard release an important document about education in America?', 'answer': '1945'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
