15:33:13 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\nWhere was Martin Luther born?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 1: Martin Luther was born in Eisleben, Saxony, in the Holy Roman Empire on November 10, 1483.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2:  When was Martin Luther baptized?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: Martin Luther was baptized the next morning after his birth, on the feast day of St. Martin of Tours.', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 3:  What were Hans Luther's ambitions for his eldest son, Martin?", 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 3: Hans Luther wanted his eldest son, Martin, to become a lawyer.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 4:  How did Hans Luther plan to achieve his ambition for Martin?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: Hans Luther planned to send Martin to Latin schools in Mansfeld, Magdeburg, and Eisenach, where the curriculum was focused on the "trivium" – grammar, rhetoric, and logic.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 5:  What was the nature of the education that Martin Luther received in Eisenach?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 5: Martin Luther received education at a school in Eisenach operated by the Brethren of the Common Life.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 6:  How did Martin Luther later describe his education in Eisenach?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 6: Martin Luther later described his education in Eisenach as resembling "purgatory and hell".', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 7:  What did Martin Luther's birth name come from?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 7: Martin Luther's birth name came from the feast day of St. Martin of Tours, on which he was baptized the next morning after his birth.", 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 8:  Did Martin Luther have any siblings?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 8: Yes, Martin Luther had several brothers and sisters.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 9:  What was the relationship between Martin Luther and his brother Jacob?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 9: Martin Luther was close to his brother Jacob.', "Martin Luther\nQuestion 10:  Which religious scholar describes Luther's mother as having trade class origins?", "Martin Luther\nAnswer 10: The religious scholar Martin Marty describes Luther's mother as having trade class origins.", 'Martin Luther\nMartin Luther was born to Hans Luder (or Ludher, later Luther) and his wife Margarethe (née Lindemann) on 10 November 1483 in Eisleben, Saxony, then part of the Holy Roman Empire. He was baptized as a Catholic the next morning on the feast day of St. Martin of Tours. His family moved to Mansfeld in 1484, where his father was a leaseholder of copper mines and smelters and served as one of four citizen representatives on the local council. The religious scholar Martin Marty describes Luther\'s mother as a hard-working woman of "trading-class stock and middling means" and notes that Luther\'s enemies later wrongly described her as a whore and bath attendant. He had several brothers and sisters, and is known to have been close to one of them, Jacob. Hans Luther was ambitious for himself and his family, and he was determined to see Martin, his eldest son, become a lawyer. He sent Martin to Latin schools in Mansfeld, then Magdeburg in 1497, where he attended a school operated by a lay group called the Brethren of the Common Life, and Eisenach in 1498. The three schools focused on the so-called "trivium": grammar, rhetoric, and logic. Luther later compared his education there to purgatory and hell.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Martin Luther was born to Hans Luder (or Ludher, later Luther) and his wife Margarethe (née Lindemann) on 10 November 1483 in Eisleben, Saxony, then part of the Holy Roman Empire. He was baptized as a Catholic the next morning on the feast day of St. Martin of Tours. His family moved to Mansfeld in 1484, where his father was a leaseholder of copper mines and smelters and served as one of four citizen representatives on the local council. The religious scholar Martin Marty describes Luther\'s mother as a hard-working woman of "trading-class stock and midd