15:38:04 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["European Union law\nDefine 'ordinary legislative procedure'.", 'European Union law\nAnswer 1: The \'ordinary legislative procedure\' according to TFEU article 294, is the process to make new legislation. It entails three readings starting with a proposal from the Commission. The Parliament must vote, by a majority of all MEPs to approve, or propose changes, and the Council must vote, by qualified majority to approve changes, but by unanimity to block changes from the Commission. If the institutions fail to agree at any stage, a "Conciliation Committee" is formed. This Committee comprises MEPs, ministers, and the Commission representatives to get a joint text. This joint text is then sent back to the Parliament and Council to be approved by absolute and qualified majority respectively.', "European Union law\nQuestion 2: How can the 'ordinary legislative procedure' be blocked?", "European Union law\nAnswer 2: The 'ordinary legislative procedure' can be blocked by a majority in Parliament (the European Parliament), a minority in the Council (the Council of the European Union), and a majority in the Commission (the European Commission).", "European Union law\nQuestion 3: What is the procedure for changing EU law using 'ordinary legislative procedure'?", "European Union law\nAnswer 3: Changing EU law using 'ordinary legislative procedure' is harder to do than merely making the law the same. This means the legislation cannot be blocked by a majority in the European Parliament, a minority in the Council, and a majority in the Commission.", 'European Union law\nQuestion 4: What happens in cases of enhanced cooperation among a sub-set of at least member states?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 4: For enhanced cooperation among at least some member states, approval must be given by the Council. The Commission should inform the member state governments about the proposals at the start of the procedure.', 'European Union law\nQuestion 5: Can you explain TEU articles 4 and 5 regarding the powers of the EU and member states?', 'European Union law\nAnswer 5: TEU articles 4 and 5 state that powers remain with the member states unless they have been conferred. However, there is a debate about the "competence" question: who ultimately decides the EU\'s competence. Many member state courts, member state Parliaments, and the Court of Justice of the EU all have differing views on the decision. The EU can only act within its power set out in the Treaties.', 'European Union law\nTo make new legislation, TFEU article 294 defines the "ordinary legislative procedure" that applies for most EU acts. The essence is there are three readings, starting with a Commission proposal, where the Parliament must vote by a majority of all MEPs (not just those present) to block or suggest changes, and the Council must vote by qualified majority to approve changes, but by unanimity to block Commission amendment. Where the different institutions cannot agree at any stage, a "Conciliation Committee" is convened, representing MEPs, ministers and the Commission to try and get agreement on a joint text: if this works, it will be sent back to the Parliament and Council to approve by absolute and qualified majority. This means, legislation can be blocked by a majority in Parliament, a minority in the Council, and a majority in the Commission: it is harder to change EU law than stay the same. A different procedure exists for budgets. For "enhanced cooperation" among a sub-set of at least member states, authorisation must be given by the Council. Member state governments should be informed by the Commission at the outset before any proposals start the legislative procedure. The EU as a whole can only act within its power set out in the Treaties. TEU articles 4 and 5 state that powers remain with the member states unless they have been conferred, although there is a debate about the Kompetenz-Kompetenz question: who ultimately has the "competence" to define the EU\'s "competence". Many member state courts believe they decide, other member state Parliaments believe they decide, while within the EU, the Court of Justice believes it has the final say.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'To make new legislation, TFEU article 294 defines the "ordinary legislative procedure" that applies for most EU acts. The essence is there are three readings, starting with a Commission proposal, where the Parliament must vote by a majority of all MEPs (not just those present) to block or suggest changes, and the Council must vote by qualified majority to approve changes, but by unanimity to block Commission amendment. Where the different institutions cannot agree at any stage, a "Conciliation Committee" is convened, representing MEPs, ministers and the Commission to try and get agreement on a joint text: if this works, it will be sent back to the Parliament and Council to approve by absolute and qualified majority. This means, legislation can be blocked by a majority in Parliament, a minority in the Council, and a majority in the Commission: it is harder to change EU law than stay the same. A different procedure exists for budgets. For "enhanced cooperation" among a sub-set of at least member states, authorisation must be given by the Council. Member state governments should be informed by the Commission at the outset before any proposals start the legislative procedure. The EU as a whole can only act within its power set out in the Treaties. TEU articles 4 and 5 state that powers remain with the member states unless they have been conferred, although there is a debate about the Kompetenz-Kompetenz question: who ultimately has the "competence" to define the EU\'s "competence". Many member state courts believe they decide, other member state Parliaments believe they decide, while within the EU, the Court of Justice believes it has the final say.', 'question': "Topic: European Union law\nWhat type of vote must the Parliament have to either block or suggest changes to the Commission's proposals?", 'answer': 'a majority'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'To make new legislation, TFEU article 294 defines the "ordinary legislative procedure" that applies for most EU acts. The essence is there are three readings, starting with a Commission proposal, where the Parliament must vote by a majority of all MEPs (not just those present) to block or suggest changes, and the Council must vote by qualified majority to approve changes, but by unanimity to block Commission amendment. Where the different institutions cannot agree at any stage, a "Conciliation Committee" is convened, representing MEPs, ministers and the Commission to try and get agreement on a joint text: if this works, it will be sent back to the Parliament and Council to approve by absolute and qualified majority. This means, legislation can be blocked by a majority in Parliament, a minority in the Council, and a majority in the Commission: it is harder to change EU law than stay the same. A different procedure exists for budgets. For "enhanced cooperation" among a sub-set of at least member states, authorisation must be given by the Council. Member state governments should be informed by the Commission at the outset before any proposals start the legislative procedure. The EU as a whole can only act within its power set out in the Treaties. TEU articles 4 and 5 state that powers remain with the member states unless they have been conferred, although there is a debate about the Kompetenz-Kompetenz question: who ultimately has the "competence" to define the EU\'s "competence". Many member state courts believe they decide, other member state Parliaments believe they decide, while within the EU, the Court of Justice believes it has the final say.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nWhat type of vote must the Council pass in order to approve of any changes recommended by Parliament?', 'answer': 'qualified majority'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'To make new legislation, TFEU article 294 defines the "ordinary legislative procedure" that applies for most EU acts. The essence is there are three readings, starting with a Commission proposal, where the Parliament must vote by a majority of all MEPs (not just those present) to block or suggest changes, and the Council must vote by qualified majority to approve changes, but by unanimity to block Commission amendment. Where the different institutions cannot agree at any stage, a "Conciliation Committee" is convened, representing MEPs, ministers and the Commission to try and get agreement on a joint text: if this works, it will be sent back to the Parliament and Council to approve by absolute and qualified majority. This means, legislation can be blocked by a majority in Parliament, a minority in the Council, and a majority in the Commission: it is harder to change EU law than stay the same. A different procedure exists for budgets. For "enhanced cooperation" among a sub-set of at least member states, authorisation must be given by the Council. Member state governments should be informed by the Commission at the outset before any proposals start the legislative procedure. The EU as a whole can only act within its power set out in the Treaties. TEU articles 4 and 5 state that powers remain with the member states unless they have been conferred, although there is a debate about the Kompetenz-Kompetenz question: who ultimately has the "competence" to define the EU\'s "competence". Many member state courts believe they decide, other member state Parliaments believe they decide, while within the EU, the Court of Justice believes it has the final say.', 'question': 'Topic: European Union law\nIs it easier or harder to change EU law than stay the same?', 'answer': 'harder'}, {'title': 'European Union law', 'context': 'To make new legislation, TFEU article 294 defines the "ordinary