15:40:33 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['French and Indian War\nIn his extensively detailed report, Céloron wrote, "All I can say is that the Natives of these localities are very badly disposed towards the French, and are entirely devoted to the English. I don\'t know in what way they could be brought back." In what regard were the natives disposed towards the French and the English?', 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 1: The natives were badly disposed towards the French and entirely devoted to the English.', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 2: Even before his return to Montreal, reports on the situation in the Ohio Country were making their way to London and Paris, each side proposing that action be taken. Which governor was particularly forceful in this proposal?', 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 2: The expansionist governor of the Province of Massachusetts Bay, William Shirley, was particularly forceful in proposing that action be taken.', 'French and Indian War\nQuestion 3: Conflicts between the colonies, accomplished through raiding parties that included Indian allies, had taken place for decades. What was the consequence of these conflicts?', 'French and Indian War\nAnswer 3: The consequence of these conflicts was the brisk trade in European colonial captives from either side.', 'French and Indian War\nCan you please summarize the main outcome of the French and Indian War?', 'French and Indian War\nDuring the French and Indian War, the British emerged victorious and France was forced to relinquish control over French Canada to the British. The war also led to significant territorial changes, with the British gaining control over the Ohio Country and the Mississippi River valley, which were areas of interest to both France and Great Britain. Additionally, the war marked the end of French colonial power in North America and led to increased British dominance in the region.', 'French and Indian War\nCan you explain the significance of the Ohio 