16:10:54 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\nWhat does Luther believe about the law?', "Martin Luther\nAnswer 1: Luther believes that the law, in any form, should still be preached to Christians as it serves as a tool for the Holy Spirit to work sorrow over sin in man's heart, thus preparing him for Christ's fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel.", 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 2: How does Luther define the "second use of the law"?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 2: Luther defines the "second use of the law" as the law being the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 3: What does Luther say about Christians and their relationship to the law?', 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 3: Luther believes that Christians are still sinners in themselves, and therefore the law – in any form – should still be preached among them.', 'Martin Luther\nQuestion 4: What does Luther believe about the relationship between Christians and the law?', "Martin Luther\nAnswer 4: Luther believes that Christians should still hear the law preached to them, as the law serves to remind them of their sins and prepares them for Christ's fulfillment of the law.", "Martin Luther\nQuestion 5: What is Luther's criticism of those who refuse to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians?", 'Martin Luther\nAnswer 5: Luther criticizes those who refuse to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians, claiming that such actions would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'Martin Luther\nIn his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of