mans, and even Webb to defend themselves, and led to a delay in the official announcement of the LOR to the press. Despite the opposition, Webb still hedged his bets, making the decision "tentative."', 'Apollo program\nThis revised script is more concise and clearer in explaining the situation. It also shows the hurdles NASA faced in getting the Apollo program off the ground, and how they had to work tirelessly to push it forward despite opposition from Kennedy\'s science advisor. Additionally, the mention of Jerome Wiesner\'s opposition earlier in the sentence helps to establish his significance as an influential figure in the decision to go to the Moon. Finally, the use of "tentative" makes clear that while there was already internal agreement, NASA was still in the process of addressing the opposition, and that the decision had yet to be finalized.', 'Apollo program\nCan you modify the previous script by adding more detailed information about the lunar landing rendezvous (LOR) and its significance in the Apollo program?', 'Apollo program\nCertainly! Here is a revised version of the previous script that includes more detailed information about the lunar landing rendezvous (LOR) and its significance in the Apollo program:', 'Apollo program\nApollo program', 'Apollo program\nBut even after NASA reached internal agreement, the process was not without its challenges. Kennedy\'s science advisor Jerome Wiesner, who had previously expressed his opposition to manned spaceflight, hired Golovin, who had left NASA, to second-guess NASA\'s decisions on the Saturn V launch vehicle and lunar landing rendezvous (LOR). This forced Shea, Seamans, and even Webb to defend themselves, and led to a delay in the official announcement of the LOR to the press. The lunar landing rendezvous (LOR) was a key component of the Apollo program, and involved the rendezvous and docking of an Apollo spacecraft with an orbiting lunar module. The success of the LOR would be critical to the program\'s ultimate success, since it would be necessary for landing men on the Moon and returning them safely to Earth. Despite the opposition, Webb still hedged his bets, making the decision "tentative."', 'Apollo program\nBut even after NASA reached internal agreement, it was far from smooth sailing. Kennedy\'s science advisor Jerome Wiesner, who had expressed his opposition to manned spaceflight to Kennedy before the President took office, and had opposed the decision to land men on the Moon, hired Golovin, who had left NASA, to chair his own "Space Vehicle Panel", ostensibly to monitor, but actually to second-guess NASA\'s decisions on the Saturn V launch vehicle and LOR by forcing Shea, Seamans, and even Webb to defend themselves, delaying its formal announcement to the press on July 11, 1962, and forcing Webb to still hedge the decision as "tentative".'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'But even after NASA reached internal agreement, it was far from smooth sailing. Kennedy\'s science advisor Jerome Wiesner, who had expressed his opposition to manned spaceflight to Kennedy before the President took office, and had opposed the decision to land men on the Moon, hired Golovin, who had left NASA, to chair his own "Space Vehicle Panel", ostensibly to monitor, but actually to second-guess NASA\'s decisions on the Saturn V launch vehicle and LOR by forcing Shea, Seamans, and even Webb to defend themselves, delaying its formal announcement to the press on July 11, 1962, and forcing Webb to still hedge the decision as "tentative".', 'question': "Topic: Apollo program\nWho was Kennedy's science adviser that opposed manned spacecraft flights?", 'answer': 'Jerome Wiesner'}, {'title': 'Apollo program', 'context': 'But even after NASA reached internal agreement, it was far from smooth sailing. Kennedy\'s science advisor Jerome Wiesner, who had expressed his opposition to manned spaceflight to Kennedy before the President took office, and had opposed the dec