16:19:21 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 1: How many LEA-funded 11 to 18 schools and independent schools with sixth forms are in Newcastle upon Tyne?\nAnswer 1: There are eleven LEA-funded 11 to 18 schools and seven independent schools with sixth forms in Newcastle upon Tyne.', 'Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 2: Name two large co-ed independent schools in Newcastle upon Tyne.\nAnswer 2: The Royal Grammar School and Newcastle High School for Girls are both large co-ed independent schools in Newcastle upon Tyne.\n', 'Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 3: What is the primary difference between LEA-funded schools and independent schools in Newcastle upon Tyne?\nAnswer 3: LEA-funded schools in Newcastle upon Tyne are run by the local education authority and are funded through public tax, while independent schools are privately funded and operate independently of the education authority.\n', "Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 4: Name one state school that has a successful sixth form in Newcastle upon Tyne.\nAnswer 4: Gosforth High School, Heaton Manor School, St Cuthbert's High School, St Mary's Catholic Comprehensive School, Kenton School, George Stephenson High School, Sacred Heart, and Benfield School all have successful sixth forms in Newcastle upon Tyne.\n", 'Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 5: What is the largest general further education college in the Newcastle area?\nAnswer 5: Newcastle College is the largest general further education college in the Newcastle area, and it is also a beacon status college.\n', "Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 6: Name two primary state-Catholic run high schools in Newcastle upon Tyne.\nAnswer 6: St Cuthbert's High School and Sacred Heart are two primary state-Catholic run high schools in Newcastle upon Tyne.\n", 'Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 7: Where are the large co-ed independent schools located in Newcastle upon Tyne?\nAnswer 7: Both the Royal Grammar School and Newcastle High School for Girls are located on the same street in Jesmond, which is in Newcastle upon Tyne.\n', "Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 8: Where is the only independent boys' only school in Newcastle upon Tyne?\nAnswer 8: Newcastle School for Boys is the only independent boys' only school in Newcastle upon Tyne, and it is located in Gosforth.\n", 'Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 9: What is a "beacon status" college?\nAnswer 9: A "beacon status" college is a designation given to colleges that have achieved a high standard of education and excellence, and it reflects that the college has a strong track record of providing high-quality education and training.\n', "Newcastle upon Tyne\nQuestion 10: What is the purpose of the passage?\nAnswer 10: The purpose of the passage is to provide information about the education system in Newcastle upon Tyne, including the different types of schools and colleges available, their locations, and some of the notable schools and colleges in the city.\n\nThank you for that detailed passage, can you please give me more information about the different types of courses offered at the Newcastle College?\nAnswer: Newcastle College offers a wide range of courses at both further education and higher education levels. At the further education level, they offer courses in the following areas:\n\n- Business and Enterprise\n- Creative Industries\n- Computing and IT\n- Engineering and Manufacturing\n- Health and Social Care\n- Modern Languages\n- Performing Arts\n- Professional Development and Training\n- Travel and Tourism\n- Virtual Learning Environment\n\nAt the higher education level, they offer a variety of degree-level programs in partnership with higher education institutions. These include:\n\n- Bachelor of Science (BSc) in Computing and IT\n- Bachelor of Science (BSc) in Engineering\n- Bachelor of Business Administration (BBA) in Business and Finance\n- Bachelor of Arts (BA) in Creative Industries\n\nIn addition to these programs, Newcastle College also offers a variety of short courses, masterclasses, and workshops for those who are looking to further their education and training. Overall, the college provides a broad range of courses to meet the needs of a diverse range of learners.\n\nCan you tell me more about the degree-level programs offered in partnership with higher education institutions?\nAnswer: Newcastle College partners with a range of higher education institutions to offer a variety of degree-level programs. Some of the degree programs available are:\n\n- Bachelor of Science (BSc) in Computing and IT offered in partnership with Teesside University\n- Bachelor of Science (BSc) in Engineering offered in partnership with Northumbria University\n- Bachelor of Business Administration (BBA) in Business and Finance offered in partnership with Newcastle University\n- Bachelor of Arts (BA) in Creative Industries offered in partnership with Northumbria University\n\nThese programs typically consist of modules and assessments taken over a period of three years, resulting in the awarding of a degree by the partner university. The degree programs are designed to provide students with a strong academic grounding in their chosen subject area and to prepare them for careers in their field of study.\n\nCan you provide more information about the health and social care courses offered at Newcastle College?\nAnswer: Newcastle College offers a range of health and social care courses at both further education and higher education levels. Some of the courses include:\n\n- Level 2 and 3 Diploma in Health and Social Care\n- Level 3 Certificate in Children and Young People's Workforce\n- Level 3 Certificate in Adult Care Workforce\n- Level 3 Certificate in Vocational Studies\n- Level 3 Diploma in Health and Social Care (Extended)\n- Level 3 Diploma in Health and Social Care (e-learning)\n- Higher National Certificate in Health and Social Care (HNC)\n- Higher National Diploma in Health and Social Care (HND)\n\nThese courses offer a practical and theoretical approach to learning, providing learners with the skills and knowledge needed to work in the health and social care sector. The courses may cover topics such as the principles of health and social care, the care of vulnerable adults, child development, and working with families. The course content can be delivered through a range of teaching methods, including online learning, classroom learning, and practical sessions.", "Newcastle upon Tyne\nThere are eleven LEA-funded 11 to 18 schools and seven independent schools with sixth forms in Newcastle. There are a number of successful state schools, including Walker Technology College, Gosforth High School, Heaton Manor School, St Cuthbert's High School, St. Mary's Catholic Comprehensive School, Kenton School, George Stephenson High School, Sacred Heart and Benfield School. The largest co-ed independent school is the Royal Grammar School. The largest girls' independent school is Newcastle High School for Girls. Both schools are located on the same street in Jesmond. Newcastle School for Boys is the only independent boys' only school in the city and is situated in Gosforth. Newcastle College is the largest general further education college in the North East and is a beacon status college; there are two smaller colleges in the Newcastle area. St Cuthbert's High School and Sacred Heart are the two primary state-Catholic run high schools, and are both achieving results on par with the independent schools in Newcastle."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Newcastle upon Tyne', 'context': "There are eleven LEA-funded 11 to 18 schools and seven independent schools with sixth forms in Newcastle. There are a number of successful state schools, including Walker Technology College, Gosforth High School, Heaton Manor School, St Cuthbert's High School, St. Mary's Catholic Comprehensive School, Kenton School, George Stephenson High School, Sacred Heart and Benfield School. The largest co-ed independent school is the R