16:21:44 [INFO] root - TRAIN[0] University of Chicago
What are the Chicago University research institutes and centers?
16:21:44 [INFO] root - TRAIN[1] University of Chicago
Answer: The University of Chicago operates 12 research institutes and 113 research centers on its campus. This includes the Oriental Institute, which is a museum and research center for Near Eastern studies owned and operated by the university, and several National Resource Centers like the Center for Middle Eastern Studies. Additionally, the university manages or has a joint stake in several external research institutions. These include Argonne National Laboratory, part of the Department of Energy's national laboratory system, Fermilab, a particle physics laboratory located nearby, and the Apache Point Observatory in Sunspot, New Mexico. In 2013, the university announced its affiliation with the Marine Biological Laboratory in Woods Hole, Massachusetts. Although not formally affiliated, the National Opinion Research Center is located on the University of Chicago campus.
16:21:44 [INFO] root - TRAIN[2] University of Chicago
The university operates 12 research institutes and 113 research centers on campus. Among these are the Oriental Institute—a museum and research center for Near Eastern studies owned and operated by the university—and a number of National Resource Centers, including the Center for Middle Eastern Studies. Chicago also operates or is affiliated with a number of research institutions apart from the university proper. The university partially manages Argonne National Laboratory, part of the United States Department of Energy's national laboratory system, and has a joint stake in Fermilab, a nearby particle physics laboratory, as well as a stake in the Apache Point Observatory in Sunspot, New Mexico. Faculty and students at the adjacent Toyota Technological Institute at Chicago collaborate with the university, In 2013, the university announced that it was affiliating the formerly independent Marine Biological Laboratory in Woods Hole, Mass. Although formally unrelated, the National Opinion Research Center is located on Chicago's campus.
