16:22:38 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Islamism\nQuestion 1: Where did Iqbal learn law and philosophy?\n\nAnswer:  Iqbal learned law and philosophy in England and Germany.', 'Islamism\nQuestion 2:  When did Iqbal become a member of the London branch of the All India Muslim League?\n\nAnswer:  Iqbal became a member of the London branch of the All India Muslim League while studying law and philosophy in England and Germany.\n', 'Islamism\nQuestion 3:  What did Iqbal do after returning to Lahore in 1908?\n\nAnswer:  After returning to Lahore in 1908, Iqbal divided his time between law practice and philosophical poetry, while remaining active in the Muslim League.\n', 'Islamism\nQuestion 4:  Why did Iqbal remain critical of the mainstream Indian nationalist and secularist Indian National Congress?\n\nAnswer:  Iqbal remained critical of the mainstream Indian nationalist and secularist Indian National Congress because he did not support Indian involvement in World War I.\n', "Islamism\nQuestion 5:  When were Iqbal's seven English lectures published in a book titled The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam?\n\nAnswer:  Iqbal's seven English lectures were published in a book titled The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam by Oxford University press in 1934.", "Islamism\nWhile studying law and philosophy in England and Germany, Iqbal became a member of the London branch of the All India Muslim League. He came back to Lahore in 1908. While dividing his time between law practice and philosophical poetry, Iqbal had remained active in the Muslim League. He did not support Indian involvement in World War I and remained in close touch with Muslim political leaders such as Muhammad Ali Johar and Muhammad Ali Jinnah. He was a critic of the mainstream Indian nationalist and secularist Indian National Congress. Iqbal's seven English lectures were published by Oxford University press in 1934 in a book titled The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam. These lectures dwell on the role of Islam as a religion as well as a political and legal philosophy in the modern age."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Islamism', 'context': "While studying law and philosophy in England and Germany, Iqbal became a member of the London branch of the All India Muslim League. He came back to Lahore in 1908. While dividing his time between law practice and philosophical poetry, Iqbal had remained active in the Muslim League. He did not support Indian involvement in World War I and remained in close touch with Muslim political leaders such as Muhammad Ali Johar and Muhammad Ali Jinnah. He was a critic of the mainstream Indian nationalist and secularist Indian National Congress. Iqbal's seven English lectures were published by Oxford University press in 1934 in a book titled The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam. These lectures dwell on the role of Islam as a religion as well as a political and legal philosophy in the modern age.", 'question': 'Topic: Islamism\nWhat was Iqbal studying in England and Germany?', 'answer': 'law and philosophy'}, {'title': 'Islamism', 'context': "While studying law and philosophy in England and Germany, Iqbal became a member of the London branch of the All India Muslim League. He came back to Lahore in 1908. While dividing his time between law practice and philosophical poetry, Iqbal had remained active in the Muslim League. He did not support Indian involvement in World War I and remained in close touch with Muslim political leaders such as Muhammad Ali Johar and Muhammad Ali Jinnah. He was a critic of the mainstream Indian nationalist and secularist Indian National Congress. Iqbal's seven English lectures were published by Oxford University press in 1934 in a book titled The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam. These lectures dwell on the role of Islam as a religion as well as a political and legal philosophy in the modern age.", 'question': 'Topic: Islamism\nWhat organizatio