ic reticulum. They synthesize ordinary starch, which is stored in granules found in the periplastid space—outside the original double membrane, in the place that corresponds to the red alga's cytoplasm. Inside cryptophyte chloroplasts is a pyrenoid and thylakoids in stacks of two.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat part of cryptophyte chloroplasts is similar to chlorarachniophytes?', 'answer': 'nucleomorph'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "Cryptophytes, or cryptomonads are a group of algae that contain a red-algal derived chloroplast. Cryptophyte chloroplasts contain a nucleomorph that superficially resembles that of the chlorarachniophytes. Cryptophyte chloroplasts have four membranes, the outermost of which is continuous with the rough endoplasmic reticulum. They synthesize ordinary starch, which is stored in granules found in the periplastid space—outside the original double membrane, in the place that corresponds to the red alga's cytoplasm. Inside cryptophyte chloroplasts is a pyrenoid and thylakoids in stacks of two.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhere do cryptophyte chloroplasts store starch?', 'answer': 'in granules found in the periplastid space'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "Cryptophytes, or cryptomonads are a group of algae that contain a red-algal derived chloroplast. Cryptophyte chloroplasts contain a nucleomorph that superficially resembles that of the chlorarachniophytes. Cryptophyte chloroplasts have four membranes, the outermost of which is continuous with the rough endoplasmic reticulum. They synthesize ordinary starch, which is stored in granules found in the periplastid space—outside the original double membrane, in the place that corresponds to the red alga's cytoplasm. Inside cryptophyte chloroplasts is a pyrenoid and thylakoids in stacks of two.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nHow do cryptophyte chloroplasts arrange their pyrenoid and thylakoids?', 'answer': 'stacks of two'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lor