16:38:17 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['University of Chicago\nWhat is the University of Chicago known for?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 1: The University of Chicago is known for shaping ideas about the free market through the Chicago school of economics, being the namesake of the Chicago school of sociology, and making significant contributions in physics, including the first self-sustained nuclear reaction and discovering REM sleep.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 2: What notable experiments and academic movements have been conducted at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 2: The University of Chicago has been the site of several important experiments and academic movements, including the Chicago school of economics, the Chicago school of sociology, the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction (Chicago Pile-1), the oil-drop experiment by Robert Millikan, the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby, the Miller–Urey experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, and the discovery of REM sleep by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 3: Who was involved in conducting the experiments and academic movements at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 3: Several notable figures were involved in conducting these experiments and academic movements at the University of Chicago, including Milton Friedman, the economist who supported the Chicago school of economics, Robert Millikan, the physicist responsible for the oil-drop experiment, Willard F. Libby, the chemist behind radiocarbon dating, and Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky, who discovered REM sleep.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 4: How did the University of Chicago shape ideas about the free market?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 4: The University of Chicago played a significant role in shaping ideas about the free market through the Chicago school of economics, which was supported by economist Milton Friedman and other economists. This school of economic thought emphasized the importance of a free market economy and the role of government in promoting economic growth and efficiency.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 5: When and how did the University of Chicago first discover REM sleep?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 5: The University of Chicago first discovered REM sleep in 1953 through the research conducted by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky. This discovery was a significant breakthrough in the understanding of sleep patterns and their relationship to dreams and the human body.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 6: What was the significance of the oil-drop experiment conducted at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 6: The oil-drop experiment conducted at the University of Chicago by Robert Millikan was a significant experiment in physics. It demonstrated the importance of the electron charge and contributed to the development of a deeper understanding of atomic and molecular processes.', 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 7: What was the significance of the Miller–Urey experiment conducted at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 7: The Miller–Urey experiment conducted at the University of Chicago was significant because it tested the origin of life on early Earth. This experiment provided valuable insights into the role of chemicals in the development of life and helped to shape our understanding of the origins of life.', "University of Chicago\nQuestion 8: How did the University of Chicago's sociology department impact the field of sociology?", "University of Chicago\nAnswer 8: The University of Chicago's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States. It gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology, a movement that emphasized the importance of studying social processes and the relationship between individuals and their social environment. This school of sociology continues to influence and shape the field of sociology today.", 'University of Chicago\nQuestion 9: What was the significance of the Chicago Pile-1 at the University of Chicago?', 'University of Chicago\nAnswer 9: The Chicago Pile-1 was the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction conducted at the University of Chicago, part of the Manhattan Project. This experiment demonstrated the potential of nuclear energy and played a crucial role in advancing our understanding of nuclear physics.', "University of Chicago\nThe University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The university's sociology department was the first independent sociology department in the United States and gave birth to the Chicago school of sociology. In physics, the university was the site of the Chicago Pile-1 (the first self-sustained man-made nuclear reaction, part of the Manhattan Project), of Robert Millikan's oil-drop experiment that calculated the charge of the electron, and of the development of radiocarbon dating by Willard F. Libby in 1947. The chemical experiment that tested how life originated on early Earth, the Miller–Urey experiment, was conducted at the university. REM sleep was discovered at the university in 1953 by Nathaniel Kleitman and Eugene Aserinsky."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'University of Chicago', 'context': "The University of Chicago has been the site of some important experiments and academic movements. In economics, the university has played an important role in shaping ideas about the free market and is the namesake of the Chicago school of economics, the school of economic thought supported by Milton Friedman and other economists. The unive