16:46:33 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Teacher\n1.What is the most common name given to teachers of Dharma in Tibet?', 'Teacher\nAnswer 1: A Lama is the most common name given to teachers of Dharma in Tibet.', 'Teacher\nQuestion 2: 2.What is a Lama who has consciously determined to be reborn for multiple times called?', 'Teacher\nAnswer 2: A Lama who has consciously determined to be reborn for multiple times is called a Tulku.', 'Teacher\nQuestion 3: 3.How is a Lama able to know that they will be reborn?', 'Teacher\nAnswer 3: A Lama is able to know that they will be reborn through phowa and siddhi.', 'Teacher\nQuestion 4: 4.What is the reason a Lama decides to be reborn multiple times?', 'Teacher\nAnswer 4: A Lama decides to be reborn multiple times in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow.', "Teacher\nCan you elaborate on the role of phowa and siddhi in the context of a Lama's rebirth?", "Teacher\nYes, certainly. Phowa and siddhi are two important elements in Tibetan Buddhism that play a critical role in a Lama's rebirth.", 'Teacher\nPhowa is a Tibetan term that refers to the "awareness of one\'s body and mind as a vehicle for achieving enlightenment." In Tibetan Buddhism, it is believed that the mind and body can play a crucial role in the attainment of spiritual goals. Phowa is a practice that enables a practitioner to purify the defilements of the mind and enhance the positive qualities within it.', 'Teacher\nSiddhi, on the other hand, refers to the "power and skill" that a practitioner acquires through the practice of phowa. Siddhi is the ability to manifest one\'s desires, wishes, and intentions. In the context of a Lama\'s rebirth, siddhi is the power and skill that they have acquired through the practice of phowa, which enables them to consciously determine their rebirth.', 'Teacher\nBy practicing phowa and siddhi, a Lama is believed to be able to transcend the cycle of birth, death, and rebirth and attain enlightenment. The Lama also wishes to continue their work towards enlightenment in their next life, and thus consciously decides to be reborn in order to achieve their goals. Through the practice of phowa and siddhi, the Lama is able to purify their mind and body, acquire the necessary skills and powers, and manifest their wishes to be reborn in a specific way.', 'Teacher\nCan you give me more examples of how phowa and siddhi are practiced in Tibetan Buddhism?', 'Teacher\nCertainly! Here are a few examples of how phowa and siddhi are practiced in Tibetan Buddhism:', "Teacher\n1. Creating a positive desire: In phowa practice, a practitioner may visualize and affirm their positive desires. This practice involves cultivating the positive emotions of compassion, generosity, and kindness, and then directing them towards oneself and others. Through this practice, a practitioner can purify their negative emotions and intentions, and develop a strong wish to achieve enlightenment for the benefit of all sentient beings. 2. Use of mantras: Siddhi practice involves the use of mantras, which are sacred sounds or words that are repeated to achieve a specific goal. In Tibetan Buddhism, mantras are used to accumulate merit and increase one's spiritual power. For example, a practitioner might recite a mantra to manifest wealth and prosperity, or to cultivate a particular virtue such as patience or compassion. 3. Altar offerings: Altar offerings are another important practice in Tibetan Buddhism that is used to accumulate merit and cultivate positive emotions. When making an offering, a practitioner directs positive intentions towards themselves and others, and makes an offering of whatever they believe will best help themselves and others. The practice of phowa and siddhi is deeply ingrained in Tibetan Buddhism and is practiced in many different ways. By cultivating positive emotions, directing intentions towards oneself and others, and making offerings to the deities or lamas, practitioners are able to accumulate merit, purify their minds, and develop the necessary skills and powers to achieve their spiritual goals.", 'Teacher\nIn Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama. A Lama who has through phowa and siddhi consciously determined to be reborn, often many times, in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow is called a Tulku.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Teacher', 'context': 'In Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama. A Lama who has through phowa and siddhi consciously determined to be reborn, often many times, in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow is called a Tulku.', 'question': 'Topic: Teacher\nWhat is the name of a teacher in Tibetan Buddhism?', 'answer': 'a Lama'}, {'title': 'Teacher', 'context': 'In Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama. A Lama who has through phowa and siddhi consciously determined to be reborn, often many times, in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow is called a Tulku.', 'question': 'Topic: Teacher\nWhat has a Lama determined to do?', 'answer': 'be reborn'}, {'title': 'Teacher', 'context': 'In Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama. A Lama who has through phowa and siddhi consciously determined to be reborn, often many times, in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow is called a Tulku.', 'question': 'Topic: Teacher\nWhat is the name of a Bodhisattva vow?', 'answer': 'Tulku'}, {'title': 'Teacher', 'context': 'In Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama. A Lama who has through phowa and siddhi consciously determined to be reborn, often many times, in order to continue their Bodhisattva vow is called a Tulku.', 'question': 'Topic: Teacher\nHow much has a Lama agreed to be reborn?', 'answer': 'often many times'}, {'title': 'Teacher', 'context': 'In Tibetan Buddhism the teachers of Dharma in Tibet are most commonly called a Lama.