16:49:10 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Rhine\nsince how long has the Rhine been impacted by humans?', 'Rhine\nAnswer 1: The Rhine has been impacted by humans since ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present).', 'Rhine\nQuestion 2: How did the sediment load of the Rhine increase over time?', 'Rhine\nAnswer 2: The sediment load of the Rhine increased as a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture) in the upland areas (central Germany).', 'Rhine\nQuestion 3: What was the main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta over the past 6000 years?', 'Rhine\nAnswer 3: The main process distributing sediment across the subrecent delta over the past 6000 years was overbank sedimentation, and over the past 2000 years, overbank sedimentation was replaced by lateral accretion.', 'Rhine\nQuestion 4: When did direct human impact on the delta start?', 'Rhine\nAnswer 4: Direct human impact on the delta started with peat mining.', 'Rhine\nQuestion 5: When did embankment of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries take place?', 'Rhine\nAnswer 5: Embankment of the major distributaries and damming of minor distributaries took place in the 11–13th century AD.', "Rhine\nQuestion 6: What were some measures taken to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up?", "Rhine\nAnswer 6: Measures taken to prevent the river's channels from migrating or silting up included digging canals, short cutting bends, and building groynes.", "Rhine\nSince ~3000 yr BP (= years Before Present), human impact is seen in the delta. As a result of increasing land clearance (Bronze Age agriculture), in the upland areas (central Germany), the sediment load of the Rhine has strongly increased and delta growth has sped up. This caused increased flooding and sedimentation, ending peat formation in the delta. The shifting of river channels to new locations, on the floodplain (termed avulsion), was the main process distributing sediment acro